{
  "image_name": "09f76aa83aa12345a7be634860585222.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure is a schematic of the global overturning circulation linking the atmosphere and ocean from the Atlantic, through Antarctica, to the Pacific. It shows how major wind belts and atmospheric cells over the Southern Ocean help drive water exchange between ocean basins and between the surface and deep ocean.\n\nAt the ocean surface, relatively warm subtropical or tropical waters move poleward toward Antarctica. Around Antarctica, strong wind-driven circulation and Southern Ocean upwelling bring deep waters upward. Some of this water is transformed into intermediate waters, labeled SAMW/AAIW, which spread northward near the surface, while very dense Antarctic Bottom Water (AABW) forms and sinks to the abyss, then flows into both the Atlantic and Pacific as a bottom current.\n\nThe Atlantic side highlights North Atlantic Deep Water (NADW) formation in the north, with NADW flowing southward at depth toward the Southern Ocean. Near Antarctica it connects with deep waters such as LCDW and returns upward. On the Pacific side, older deep waters appear as UCDW and PDW, which upwell near Antarctica and are returned to shallower layers.\n\nOverall, the figure emphasizes Antarctica as the central junction where winds, upwelling, water-mass transformation, and deep/bottom water formation connect the global ocean circulation.",
